Date: Fri, 5 Feb 93 20:15:56 EST
From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)
Subject: Econ: Zero-sum games

> From: david director friedman <ddfr@midway.uchicago.edu>

> The second approach is to compare, in each case, assets at time 0
> with assets at time T, and in each case ignore the opportunity cost
> to both of us combined of holding the relevant assets. The stock
> transaction is then as Perry described it. The commodities
> transaction starts out with John holding an ounce of gold and James
> $100. James buys the gold. Gold goes up to $200. The two between them
> have made a gain of $100 just as in Perry's example.
> 
> I hope this helps.

I hate to correct David, as I respect him greatly, but I don't think
he interpreted me properly. I am not refering to the commodities market.
Commodities, when traded directly, are no different from anything else,
be it stocks, bonds, houses, etc. I am refering to DERIVATIVES, meaning
futures, options, and the like.

Maybe I should take a different tack to explain my point, and avoid the
term "zero sum" for a while.

Although its true that if we are selling an actual ounce of gold that
money can be made without anyone losing money, it is not true if I am 
making a bet. The difference is this:

If I sell you a share of stock, I am now out of the transaction. If,
on the other hand, I sell you a share of stock short, should the price
rise I will find myself out of a bit of money. In a futures or options
transaction I am ALWAYS going short if I write a contract. (We have to
distinguish this from selling a contract, by the way). If I write you
a contract promising to give you an ounce of gold next tuesday, I've
effectively shorted gold -- I'm selling you something I don't have, and
if the price rises I lose money. I could, of course, own the gold,
but I needn't actually own it, and thats the key. If I own the gold,
what I've done is basically just made a fancy sort of advance sale. If,
on the other hand, I write an uncovered contract, which is what most
participants in the market do, I've taken a short position.

In the stock market, owning something means being long. Whats traded
in the market can be thought of as "long positions". In the options
market, by contrast, half the things being sold are long positions and
half are short positions. As many people at any time are short as are
long. In the stock market, the fact that I own a share of IBM doesn't
imply that someone else is betting that IBM will go down -- but
a futures contract in IBM makes exactly that implication, as the writer
of the contract will lose money if it goes up. (I'll address the
question of covered sales in a moment.)

While in the stock market I must make a side transaction, the
borrowing of stock, in order to put myself in a short position, in the
derivatives markets short positions are half the game!

Every time my shares of IBM rise, I make money without it being at
someone else's expense. Every time my gold rises in value, I make money
without it being at someone elses expense. However, an option to buy gold
at $100 is a bet -- and if gold goes to $200, I might make $100, but someone 
out there has lost money, namely the writer of the contract, who was 
effectively short.

This is borne out, by the way, by the fact that most amateur futures
traders, in industry parlance "blow up", that is, lose all their investment,
and usually very fast. On the other hand, far fewer equities or bond
positions disappear utterly.

I'm prehaps making an odd distinction here -- Robin could argue that
covered positions of either variety, derivatives or straight trading,
are effectively identical, and that uncovered positions of either variety
are identical. He'd be right, too. However, typically futures trades
are uncovered, and typically bond trades are covered. This may seem
like a petty distinction of "investment strategy" to Robin, but I'd argue
it isn't. The stock market is used to buy and sell shares of companies,
whereas, in typical usage, the options market is used to buy and sell
uncovered bets about the shares of the companies. 

Prehaps this is all just a semantic debate, but I'm trying to make a
very real point here -- if you make an uncovered trade in the futures market,
you are making a gambling bet. Nothing wrong with gambling, you
understand -- but you should know what you are doing.

Either you will make money, or the guy you are betting with will make money. 
Both of you can't make money.

People like Paul Jones end up sucking millions of dollars out of the millions
of guys who think they are smarter than average and turn out to be
stupider than average. This is not to say that they are aware of what
they are doing, you understand -- the markets track the underlying
physicals. Paul Jones will think of himself as having taken a canny
long position in the oil market -- its the hundreds of people who
took short positions, though, that are paying him at the end of the month,
not the sale of a tanker of oil. He doesn't buy tankers of oil -- he
makes good bets with people who lose.

Most investors in these markets are going to lose money -- 
a few large investors make lots of money year after year because they
are very good, and the money they make has to come from somewhere, and 
that somewhere is usually the small and foolish investor. To Nabisco, 
stability in the price of purchased wheat is worth a small loss here and 
there. To an individual like the gentleman who proposed that some list members
go into futures trading, that loss is likely not quite so acceptable.
Things would be somewhat different if the small investors were making
only covered trades or trading the actual physicals, but they aren't.
The attraction of the futures market is that theres a lot of leverage and
that you don't need to take delivery of a flock of sheep (unless you
wait too long). With this advantage comes a risk.

This is not to say that I have anything against the derivatives market
or against short selling. Its merely to say that one has to be aware
of the way that the futures market works.

> P.S. All of this is aside from utility payoffs due to risk spreading,
> and also aside from gains due to the effect of our behavior on
> other people--gold miners, entrepreneurs using the price of XYZ
> stock to decide whether to start similar companies, etc.

