Date:     Sat, 6 Feb 93 8:42:18 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject: ECON: Wilson & Hall's Claims

Andy Wilson's claim about all strip mall investors being shallow is mere
prejudice.  For some reason, such prejudice hasn't the bad rep it deserves,
unlike the bad rep of racist prejudices.  However, I've recently made it one
of my missions in life to give a worse reputation to such statist bias against
the differently financed than those against the differently-raced.  Such
bigotry hasn't any place in polite, civilized society.

That said, there is a true cost being ignored by some of us supporters of
strip malls over old downtown stores.  The old stores are well-known to
their customers; in other words, their customers have invested in info
relevant to the store.  They base their plans upon this info.  If the
store is driven out of business through superior competition, these plans
are disrupted.  This is a disequilibration of plan coordination that is
endogenous to the market process which I've managed to articulate due to
the discussion of the subject in Roy Cordato's "Welfare Economics &
Externalities," which I reccomend to you (I really should get around to
writing all the book reviews I want to!).

We mustn't ignore this cost, even though it's part of an enormously
beneficial process.

Mr. Hall seems to think our defense of Milken et al. valueless.  On the
contrary, the standard of value is human life.  Milken improved our lives
in many ways, without our ever having to have met him.  The lower prices
offered by Wal-Mart improve the lives of the communities they are in.
(Although these weren't really the comparative advantage of Wal-Mart.
Customer service was.  Sam Walton would make surprise visits to his stores
while he traveled around the country, and if they didn't have good service,
heads metaphorically rolled.)

Garrett Goebel still thinks that there's any demand for Statism.  Supply,
yes, but demand?  Only under extreme duress!  This seems to follow the
standard process identified by Groucho Marx: "Politics is the art of
looking for trouble, finding it ever, misdiagnosing it and applying the
wrong remedy."

The State has grown this way: it looks for trouble.  Whatever trouble it
finds it (mis)diagnoses as a market failure.  It then grows to allegedly
"solve" this problem, causing more trouble, which it then looks for,
finds, misdiagnoses, grows to allegedly solve, ad infinitum.

Any challenge to this process is attacked as support of the problem or
trouble.

There's an oft-overlooked distinction between Government and State,
developed at some length in Albet Jay Nock's "Our Enemy, the State."
Government is the security of rights.  Statism is the coercive monopoly
of all force, retaliatory and initiatory.

Given these, it's correct that government is a product supplied when
demanded, endogenously to the market process.  It's incorrect that Statism
is such a thing.  The process by which it grows constitutes mass fraud -
and that's in the best case scenario, in which it grows gradually, rather
than all at once in the wake of a putsch or coup d'etat.  (Which shouldn't
be confused with Revolutions.  A putsch or coup is not a revolution.)

When I first heard the above theory, I was skeptical.  It didn't seem
possible that it could have the universality claimed for it.  Surely some
of the problems were truly market failures, and were solved by Statism!

However, as I've fleshed out my knowledge of the theory with history, I've
yet to come across many true market failures which were thus solved.  One
of the most recent ones involves police.

I grew up being told that the reason we have police is to fight crime.  It
turns out that, while they may be used for this somewhat, this is not what
police were invented for, and not what they are mainly used for in many
contemporary cases.

It seems that police originated in the USA, at least, during the 1840s.
They weren't for fighting crime at all - that was what the militia was for.
They were for fighting rioters.  I suspect that most of the riots were of
strikers whose strikes had been broken by those police, too.

Where police actually originated is a question I'd like the answer to.
They don't appear until after the Enlightenment as far as I can tell.
The first secret police force seems to have been Napoleon Bonaparte's, at
least in Western history.  It developed out of the informers and secret
intrigues of the court of Louis XIV - Sartines was the Minister of this
primitive Central Intelligence Agency.

>From Bonaparte the idea of secret police was put into practice by Germany
and Austria.  I also highly suspect that they were used against the
rebels of the so-called "Revolutions of 1848" which were actually socialist
coup attempts in France, Germany, and elsewhere on the European Continent.

If my suspicions are correct, then it would seem logical both that there
would be sympathetic strikes in England and the USA, and that breaking
them would be the purpose for which police forces were adopted there as well.
I do know that it was during the American Civil War that Federal troops
were first used to break strikes (of railroad workers whose railroads had
been nationalized by Lincoln), and during which that a Federal secret
police was first created, the Secret Service (to track down counterfeiters
of the fiat currency issued by the Federal government).

I haven't put all the pieces together, but it seems highly likely that
police are just such another outgrowth of the State and nothing to do
with any demand for protection against crime.  There are plenty of other
historical examples - "affordable" housing is one example with big
monetary impact - but I won't go through them until challenged.

I am interested in any early history of police that anyone can relate to
me.

