Date: Sun, 7 Feb 93 08:21:11 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: META: Burden of proof reform

I would like to propose a means of reforming list dialogue that I think
will reduce flaming and yet encourage the open exchange of ideas. It
involves the allocation of burdens of proof, and might be said to demand
that those who challenge basic tenets of Extropianism "Back it up or back
down." 

Those of who have been on the list for a relatively long while have seen
plenty of flame wars over unsubstantiated facts.  Time and again newcomers
to the list spout off with undigested media pap: Milken was a criminal;
Microsoft is a monopoly; the FDA saves us from our own ignorance; ad
nauseam.  It's hard for an Extropian to let such rubbish pass by
unchallenged, but who has time to fight all the world's battles?  And we
shouldn't have to take on all comers on this list!  It is *our* sanctuary
from a world where deathism and statism go unquestioned, a place where we
can quietly build on our shared consensus of Extropian ideals.  At the same
time, though, we want to invite critical views of Extropianism, and new
infusions of data that may lead us to rethink basic assumptions.  How do we
strike a balance between quieting untutored mass media mouthpieces and
welcoming thoughtful, polite, and *informative* criticisms of
Extropianism's working hypotheses?

I propose that proper placement of the burden of proof can help us to solve
this dilemma.  Those who work within Extropianism's basic framework of
values and assumptions need not justify their claims.  They can
unapologetically say that death is bad news, that trees do not have rights,
and that the war on drugs must end.  But people who want to challenge these
and other tenets of Extropianism must back up their claims with citations
to outside sources that we can find and evaluate ourselves.  They bear the
burden of proof of convincing us, and must give us the informational tools
to let us deliberate independently about what they assert.  Mere references
to "common knowledge" or "I've heard it said" will not normally suffice.

I believe that incorporating this burden of proof in our list discussions
will work to all our benefit.  Those of us who basically agree with
Extropianism will enjoy a chance to set aside the fundamentals and get on
to discussing the finer points of transhumanist philosophy.  People who
disagree with our assumptions will be welcome to challenge us, however.  By
asking them to attach proof we will insure that their claims carry some
weight, that they give us the tools to consider their claims carefully, and
that they themselves carefully reconsider the status quo.

I welcome your thoughts.  Since my proposal has not yet become part of list
culture, though, I will not demand that you attach proof if you oppose my
suggestion.  ;-)


