Date: Sun, 7 Feb 93 09:02:13 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: Virtuality/PPL: "Back it up or back down"

I just posted a message to the list proposing a reform that would require
those challenging basic tenets of Extropianism to bear the burden of proof
in proving their claims.  We could thus say to challengers "Back it up or
back down!"  Now I would like to explain how I think this suggests
something interesting about the jurisdictional boundaries of virtual
communities.

I suggest that burdens of proof mark off the boundaries of Internet's
virtual communities.  The members of each virtual community, such as our
own Extropians list, accept certain working hypotheses to structure and
guide their discussions.  Those who enter such virtual communities must
either accept those hypotheses or provide documentation that works to their
refutation.

Such burdens of proof serve as analogs of property rights in real-world
PPLs.  Members of such PPLs work under the assumption that they have rights
to their persons and property that allows them to justifiably demand that
those who enter their communities either play by their rules of convince
them to bend them.

This is another manifestation of what I think must be a deep relationship
between information and property rights.  I am also exploring this
relationship as it appears in the standards that define the initial
acquisition of property.  If we initially acquire property by mixing our
labor with it, how do we limit the extent of our claims?  If I pour a
teaspoon of tomato juice in the ocean, and it dilutes throughout the whole,
can I claim the ocean as my own?  Surely not, but why?  I suggest that the
test is whether or not I have increased the information content of that
which I claim.  
 
The idea is rough but suggestive.  It hints that Extropianism points to the
foundations of property rights.  I leave the application of these ideas to
*intellectual* property as an exercise to the reader.


