Date: Sun, 7 Feb 93 15:11:45 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: Virtuality/PPL: "Back it up or back down"

Tim May seems to have misunderstood my burden of proof proposal.  I
suggested only that people who make claims expressly contrary to what
Extropians normally accept should have to provide documentation.  Tim
applies this requirement to a claims that speak neithers for nor against
Extropianism: Locke's theory of the intial acquisition of property.  He
writes:

>I suggest Tom _prove_ this claim that we "initially acquire property
>by mixing our labor with it." I know this Lockean view is popular with
>some, but I'm not totally convinced...

Tim also appears to want me to prove Locke's theory.  I never asked that
much of those who challenge Extropianism--I only want them to give the rest
of us sources so that we can independently verify their claims.  What's
more, I never even argued that Locke is right!  I merely wrote "If we
initially acquire property by mixing our labor with it . . ." and was
merely pointing to a theory that many people do in fact accept.

Tim's request doesn't upset me a bit, and I will happily humor him with a
citation to a discussion on Locke's theory:  try Locke's "Second Treatise,"
 Ch V (Of Property).  But I do want to emphasize that I am not proposing
that everyone do what Tim has asked of me.  I only ask that people who make
claims expressly contradicting Extropianism's main tenets provide
documentation that supports (but not necessarily *proves*) their claims.



