Subject: POLI: May my sister visit me?

    This is about one of those events from my private life that make me
passionately hate the governments.
 
A while ago, I sent invitiations to my father and sister to visit me in U.S.
The invitation clearly stated that I would be financially responsible for
any medical treatment or anything else they might possibly need.
 
A few days ago, the American Consulate in Russia made its decision:
my father may come visit, but not my sister. No explanations were given,
but my folks write that it is because "she's young and cute".
I do believe that; INS is known to not grant visas to anybody who has any
personal merits (including, besides cute girls, intellectuals, people
with strong English skills, etc.), unless they have left some 'hostages'
back there (e.g., my father leaves two kids in Russia when coming 
to visit, so he would have to get back).
   Personally, I consider this decision insulting, if not criminal.
Nobody has a right to prevent people from seeing each other at their
own expense.   And this is clearly a case where no illegal activity
has occured, or is expected to.
   There are only two ways my sister could use to settle in U.S.:
legal immigration and marriage.  (this all is purely hypothetical; she
wasn't planning to do either of these things. In future, maybe.
But recently, my mother died from cancer there, and we, the rest of the
family, would just like to see and hug each other).
 
  In the case of immigration, she would have to ask the same INS to allow
her to stay. If they don't want to let her in, they just won't.  
I fail to see how they may lose here.
  In case of marriage, I guess, their concern is that she might take
a chance away from somebody old and ugly, but an American citizen.
 
  But in any case, they limit her freedom to prevent her from taking
actions that 1) are legal according to their own laws and 2) they have
no proof she is going to take.
 
  I hate laws treating people according to the territory they were born in.
IMO, they are no different from fascism.
 
  And I hate the feeling of living in a prizon with limited visitation
rights.
 
   Alexander Chislenko

